<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bezeklik Thousand Buddha Caves</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bezeklik caves</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bezeklik Thousand Buddha Caves are a complex of Buddhist cave temples in the Flaming Mountains near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, dating from the 5th to the 14th century. Many were painted under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> kingdom of Qocho, with murals of Buddhist scenes and Uyghur donors. In the early 20th century German expeditions led by Albert von Le Coq removed large sections of the murals to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bezeklik-caves.jpg" alt="Bezeklik Thousand Buddha Caves" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turpan-bezeklik-cuevas-d01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Bezeklik Thousand Buddha Caves lie between the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> and Shanshan, at the north-east of the Taklamakan Desert, near the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>. They are high on the cliffs of the west Mutou Valley, a gorge in the Flaming Mountains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. The complex dates from the 5th to the 14th century, and most of the surviving caves belong to the West Uyghur kingdom, around the 10th to 13th centuries. There are 77 rock-cut caves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Most caves are rectangular chambers with round arched ceilings, often divided into four sections, each with a mural of the Buddha, so that the whole ceiling seems covered by hundreds of Buddha murals. Some murals show a large Buddha surrounded by Turks, Indians and Europeans. Their quality varies from naive work to masterpieces of religious art.</p><p>The best-known are the large Praṇidhi scenes, which show Sakyamuni's promise made in a past life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> robes appear as donors, as in Scene 6 from Temple 9, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, who took over Sogdian writing and faiths, looked to the Sogdians as mentors. The painting shows a small Indian influence but owes more to Chinese and Iranian styles, particularly Sasanian Persian landscape painting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The Buddhist Uyghurs of Qocho and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> were converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> by conquest under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> ruler Khizr Khoja, who reigned from 1389 to 1399. Their descendants later lost the memory of this heritage and believed that the Kalmuks had built the Buddhist monuments. The murals suffered considerable damage. The local Muslim population destroyed all statues, defaced some paintings and smeared others with mud, often gouging out the eyes and mouths because of a belief that the figures might otherwise come to life at night. Pieces of mural were also broken off for fertiliser.</p><p>In the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries European and Japanese explorers found intact murals buried in sand, and many were removed and dispersed. The German explorer Albert von Le Coq took some of the best-preserved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and was the first to study them, publishing his findings in 1913.</p><p>Large pieces, including the Praṇidhi scenes, were fixed to the walls of the Museum of Ethnology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and could not be removed for safekeeping in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, so they were destroyed when the museum was hit in bombing. Other pieces are in the Hermitage Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, the Tokyo National Museum, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> and the national museums of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bezeklik Thousand Buddha Caves” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court first favoured Manichaeism, but Buddhism became dominant, and the <b>Bezeklik caves</b> were decorated with murals of Uyghur donors.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-qocho">Kingdom of Qocho</a></li></ul></section>
